This test verifies that a copy (comdb2ar) is aborted if the database runs a full
recovery while the copy is in flight, and that it survives exclusive operations
that don't.

A full recovery unwinds at least one commit, rewriting data pages backwards, so
the pages captured by an overlapping copy no longer reconcile with the forward
log replay the archive relies on.  Skip-recovery (a truncate that unwinds no
commit, the common replicant and physrep case after an election) only drops log
whose pages were never written, which the copy can safely hold.

The copy (logdelete4) holds the recovery lock and the bdb lock in read mode.
When recovery or an upgrade/downgrade waits on one of them, the copy releases
them and takes them back afterwards.  Each full recovery bumps a generation, and
copy_complete answers "aborted" only if that generation moved during the copy.

The test, for BOTH online_recovery on and off:
  1. Connects a copy and holds it at connect (debug_block_comdb2ar) after it has
     taken the read locks.
  2. Forces a rewind on the master with sys.cmd.truncate_time, which unwinds an
     applied insert (masters never skip recovery).
  3. Releases the copy and verifies comdb2ar aborts with a non-zero exit,
     reporting that recovery ran during the copy.
  4. Also verifies a copy with no concurrent recovery still succeeds.
Then, on a cluster:
  5. Holds a copy on the master, downgrades it, releases the copy, and verifies
     the copy succeeds, the node ran no full recovery, and the archive restores.
